{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from the year 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by religious conflict and political intrigue. His life story involves complex alliances with Muslim leaders as well as fierce rivalries with other Crusader nobles who often disagreed on military strategy or diplomatic approaches toward surrounding powers. Raymond inherited his title after his father died under suspicious circumstances involving the Knights Templar and this early tragedy shaped his cautious and calculating approach to governance throughout his long tenure.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nRaymond faced immediate threats upon assuming power because rival factions sought to undermine his authority while external enemies plotted against the fragile Crusader states. He spent considerable effort securing his borders and forging temporary truces with Nur ad-Din Zengi to ensure stability for his people despite pressure from more aggressive barons within the kingdom who demanded constant warfare against Islamic forces. His ability to negotiate peace treaties demonstrated a level of pragmatism that set him apart from many contemporaries who preferred martial glory over diplomatic solutions.\n\n*Regency and Political Influence*\nThe count later acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule independently without assistance from trusted advisors like Raymond. This period saw Raymond wielding immense influence over royal decisions while navigating court plots orchestrated by enemies such as Guy of Lusignan and his supporters who viewed the regent as an obstacle to their own ambitions. Raymond advocated for careful diplomacy rather than rash military action yet his counsel was sometimes ignored by factions eager for battle that ultimately led to disastrous consequences for the Christian presence in the Holy Land.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nRaymond retired from public life following the catastrophic Battle of Hattin where much of the Crusader army was destroyed and he died shortly thereafter in 1187 leaving behind a complicated legacy defined by both strategic brilliance and unfortunate timing. Historians often debate whether his warnings about Saladin were heeded too late or if the kingdom was already doomed regardless of his advice yet his contributions remain significant in understanding the decline of Crusader power in the twelfth century. His life illustrates the delicate balance required to maintain a Christian state amidst overwhelming odds and shifting political landscapes that characterized the medieval Near East during this volatile period.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.710288e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0011675615}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.006750068}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.5788944}, {"token": " year", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.1920936e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -1.180179e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -1.8596822e-05}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -5.8412725e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.0994463e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.00058584684}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.00024